t. This usually means a parse/semantic error in the spec or config.\n- module Consumer :: consec: ERROR\n    note: TLC exited with a non-zero code but no recognisable invariant or initial-predicate violation was found in its output. This usually means a parse/semantic error in the spec or config.\n- module Consumer :: property: ERROR\n    note: TLC exited with a non-zero code but no recognisable invariant or initial-predicate violation was found in its output. This usually means a parse/semantic error in the spec or config.\nFirst error line: Parsing or semantic analysis failed.\n- refinement obligation: ERROR\n    note: TLC exited with a non-zero code but no recognisable invariant or initial-predicate violation was found in its output. This usually means a parse/semantic error in the spec or config.\n\nLast bundle slug: producer_consumer_system\nParent module: ProducerConsumerSystem\nModules: BoundedQueue(abs), BoundedQueue(impl), Producer(abs), Producer(impl), Consumer(abs), Consumer(impl)\nRevise the failing module(s) and/or abstraction map(s). Return one call to repair_module_bundle with the FULL revised bundle."}]}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_01N2s1DeNk36PdNgfR5jF3Ez', 'name': 'repair_module_bundle', 'input': {'reasoning': "Multiple issues: (1) Seq(1..MaxVal) is not enumerable in TLC — can't be used as the domain of a recursive function or as a type assertion (\\in Seq(...)). Replaced with explicit bounded type predicates and converted SumSeq into a RECURSIVE operator. (2) The parent had similar issues with SumSeq[s \\in Seq(...)]. (3) Removed Seq(...) membership constraints and bounded sequence lengths via explicit predicates. (4) Cleaned up Consumer abs/impl to avoid non-enumerable sets in \\E v \\in 1..MaxVal /\\ v \\in 1..MaxVal contradictions. Also tightened parent Inv so that it doesn't use Seq(1..10) membership in checks. Also used RECURSIVE operator and bounded SumSeq computation only over actually-built received